2cc5280e-8de7-5945-a4e2-7c56b5047caa" :question/math-academy-id "q-35292" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 22 & -44 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{22}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 22 & -44 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-35292/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "004c4177-ca0e-586c-962a-1220b4b64f47"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-35292"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-34854"] :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]})=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5410"] :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/assets/c0a1d1860f5be98c6606f191fd26b0f799bc182b7b4e7fdca542d693290f11e4.png)" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form. We see that the matrix above has two pivot columns: the $1$st and the $2$nd columns. This tells us that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ forms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of two vectors, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* two vectors. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/assets/268ea5cffffb0765f5c4bbacff7f3253ad4243e0e1ffa77d3a8a5a7f794d62ba.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5411"] :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/assets/668c8e17567f5635f3d39c98dced5c77c60a287cac8a92d29db2e8afefe94cec.png)" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -15 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix above has one pivot columns: the $1$st column. This tells us that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1 \\}$ forms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of one vector, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* one vector. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/assets/4196e42d01f879b24974be9c0d1bc456c4894cb19b832efab46f3cdb8a28b644.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
